1
00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:05.271
There are many occasions when
you would like to put a human in the loop

2
00:00:05.405 --> 00:00:08.408
to keep tabs on what an agent is doing.

3
00:00:08.641 --> 00:00:11.478
This is pretty easy to do with LangGraph.

4
00:00:11.478 --> 00:00:13.613
Let's see how this works.

5
00:00:13.613 --> 00:00:16.616
We're going to resume
from where we left off in the last lesson.

6
00:00:16.716 --> 00:00:19.719
So let's start
by setting up our environment variables.

7
00:00:22.822 --> 00:00:23.390
From there,

8
00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:26.626
we can make all the relevant imports
and set up our checkpointer.

9
00:00:27.694 --> 00:00:30.230
We're now going to set up our agent state.

10
00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:33.199
And we're going to make
one small modification.

11
00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:36.069
In the previous example,

12
00:00:36.069 --> 00:00:39.272
we annotated the messages list
with the operator.add.

13
00:00:39.339 --> 00:00:43.643
That added messages
to the existing messages array.

14
00:00:44.344 --> 00:00:47.447
However, for these human-in-the-loop
interactions,

15
00:00:47.647 --> 00:00:50.717
we may want to actually replace
existing messages.

16
00:00:51.418 --> 00:00:55.889
In order to do that, we're going to write
a custom reduced messages function,

17
00:00:56.356 --> 00:00:59.359
that basically looks for messages
with the same ID,

18
00:01:00.026 --> 00:01:02.796
and if it sees that you're inserting
one with the same ID

19
00:01:02.796 --> 00:01:05.698
as one that already exists,
it's just going to replace that.

20
00:01:05.698 --> 00:01:08.301
Otherwise, it will append them.

21
00:01:08.301 --> 00:01:11.805
After that, we create our same Tavily
tool that we've been using.

22
00:01:14.007 --> 00:01:16.443
And we can create our same agent.

23
00:01:16.443 --> 00:01:19.446
We're actually going
to make one small modification.

24
00:01:19.612 --> 00:01:22.248
So when we compile the graph, in addition

25
00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:25.218
to passing in the checkpointer,

26
00:01:25.318 --> 00:01:27.287
we're also going to pass this interrupt

27
00:01:27.287 --> 00:01:30.490
before equals action parameter.

28
00:01:30.757 --> 00:01:31.691
What this is going to do

29
00:01:31.691 --> 00:01:35.428
is it's going to add an interrupt
before we call the action node.

30
00:01:35.728 --> 00:01:38.731
So the action node is
where we call the tools.

31
00:01:38.765 --> 00:01:40.266
And so the reason that we're going to do

32
00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:44.237
this, is we're going to add something
that requires manual approval.

33
00:01:44.237 --> 00:01:46.606
Before we run any tools.

34
00:01:46.606 --> 00:01:49.309
This is useful when you want to make sure
that tools are executed

35
00:01:49.309 --> 00:01:52.312
correctly.

36
00:01:52.979 --> 00:01:55.815
This interrupt happens

37
00:01:55.815 --> 00:01:59.519
before we call the action node
where all tools are called.

38
00:01:59.919 --> 00:02:03.556
Sometimes you may only want to interrupt
if a certain tool is called.

39
00:02:03.923 --> 00:02:06.426
That's covered
in other parts of the documentation,

40
00:02:06.426 --> 00:02:08.194
and I encourage
you to check that out later.

41
00:02:11.764 --> 00:02:14.134
What's
initialization with the same system

42
00:02:14.134 --> 00:02:17.137
prompt and model and checkpointer
that we've been using.

43
00:02:18.738 --> 00:02:19.839
We now call it.

44
00:02:19.839 --> 00:02:23.309
And we'll pass in this thread config
using the thread ID of one.

45
00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:26.813
Because this is a separate
notebook, it'll start from fresh.

46
00:02:28.081 --> 00:02:31.484
We stream back responses
and we stop after this

47
00:02:31.518 --> 00:02:36.122
AI message. This is because this AI message
is saying that we should call it "tool",

48
00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:39.893
but we have that interrupt
before parameter which stops it

49
00:02:39.893 --> 00:02:41.494
there.

50
00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:43.296
One thing we can do from here is

51
00:02:43.296 --> 00:02:46.299
get the current state of the graph
for this thread.

52
00:02:46.499 --> 00:02:50.236
So, in order to do that
we'll pass in this thread config.

53
00:02:51.137 --> 00:02:55.108
We'll get back this configuration object
which has a few parameters.

54
00:02:55.441 --> 00:02:58.645
We can see that the largest ones
are this list of messages here.

55
00:02:58.645 --> 00:03:00.647
This is the state of the graph.

56
00:03:00.647 --> 00:03:02.048
At this point in time.

57
00:03:04.584 --> 00:03:07.320
We can
also see that it has a next parameter.

58
00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.789
This is the node
that is to be called "next".

59
00:03:09.789 --> 00:03:11.424
We can see here that it's action.

60
00:03:11.424 --> 00:03:14.427
This means that we're about
to call the action node.

61
00:03:16.596 --> 00:03:19.599
In order to continue,
we can call stream again

62
00:03:19.599 --> 00:03:22.902
with the same thread config
and just pass in none

63
00:03:23.136 --> 00:03:26.139
as the input.

64
00:03:26.472 --> 00:03:29.342
This will stream back results
and we will see that

65
00:03:29.342 --> 00:03:32.345
we get the tool message
from calling the tool.

66
00:03:34.113 --> 00:03:37.116
And then we also get the final AI message.

67
00:03:37.684 --> 00:03:40.954
Notice that there was no break
in between the action node

68
00:03:40.954 --> 00:03:44.891
and the LLM node, because we didn't
add any interrupt there.

69
00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:48.628
If we now get the state,

70
00:03:48.628 --> 00:03:52.665
we can see that the messages
list contains the full list of messages.

71
00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:59.872
And if we get the next parameter,
we can see that it's empty.

72
00:03:59.872 --> 00:04:02.875
There's nothing left to be done.

73
00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:06.279
For fun, we can write some code
that runs this in a little loop

74
00:04:06.679 --> 00:04:09.916
and prompts us for input
about whether to continue or not.

75
00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:13.453
We'll pass in a new thread ID,
so we start afresh.

76
00:04:15.521 --> 00:04:19.025
We get this little input box here
asking us whether we want to proceed.

77
00:04:19.292 --> 00:04:20.693
We can hit yes.

78
00:04:20.693 --> 00:04:23.696
And then the agent continues on its way.

79
00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:27.166
This is a good time to stop
and try it out with other inputs.

80
00:04:27.267 --> 00:04:31.404
Try adding different places
to interrupt before and see what happens.

81
00:04:32.772 --> 00:04:34.707
Before we get to the next section,

82
00:04:34.707 --> 00:04:37.710
let's talk
a little bit more about state memory.

83
00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:42.910
As a graph is executing, a snapshot
of each state is stored in memory.

84
00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:46.052
What's in that snapshot?

85
00:04:46.052 --> 00:04:49.555
Well there's the agent state
which you've already defined.

86
00:04:50.023 --> 00:04:52.258
And then there's some other useful things.

87
00:04:52.258 --> 00:04:55.028
For example, there's a thread

88
00:04:55.028 --> 00:04:57.864
and a unique identifier
for each of the snapshots.

89
00:04:57.864 --> 00:05:00.066
That's the thread TS right there.

90
00:05:00.066 --> 00:05:03.069
You can use that to access the snapshots.

91
00:05:03.703 --> 00:05:06.506
There's some commands to access memory.

92
00:05:06.506 --> 00:05:09.409
There's get state
which you've already seen.

93
00:05:09.409 --> 00:05:13.346
If you provide the thread
without the unique identifier

94
00:05:13.346 --> 00:05:17.050
and just return that thread ID,
it will return the current state.

95
00:05:17.483 --> 00:05:21.554
There's also get state history,
which returns an iterator

96
00:05:21.854 --> 00:05:24.691
over all of the state snapshots.

97
00:05:24.691 --> 00:05:26.926
You can use the iterator to get access

98
00:05:26.926 --> 00:05:30.396
to all of the unique identifiers
for each of the states.

99
00:05:30.797 --> 00:05:32.165
What can you do with that?

100
00:05:32.165 --> 00:05:34.100
Well, here's an example.

101
00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:39.100
Given the thread identifier, or rather
that thread TS the unique identifier,

102
00:05:39.238 --> 00:05:43.176
you could, for example, access
that first state, state one,

103
00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:47.113
and use that in an invoke command.
That will use state one

104
00:05:47.113 --> 00:05:51.150
as the current state or the starting point
for the rest of the graph.

105
00:05:51.351 --> 00:05:53.553
This is effectively time travel.

106
00:05:53.553 --> 00:05:57.957
Conversely, without that thread TS,
if you just pass in the thread ID,

107
00:05:58.124 --> 00:06:01.194
it will use the current state
of the thread as the starting point.

108
00:06:01.627 --> 00:06:04.630
You can also use that unique identifier

109
00:06:04.831 --> 00:06:07.633
to access a particular state.

110
00:06:07.633 --> 00:06:10.303
You can then modify that state,

111
00:06:10.303 --> 00:06:13.740
and then you can use update state,
to update the state

112
00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:17.810
and store it back into memory
in the location of the current state.

113
00:06:18.211 --> 00:06:21.214
From there, if you run Stream or Invoke,

114
00:06:21.381 --> 00:06:25.585
it'll use the new state,
the modified state as a starting point.

115
00:06:26.285 --> 00:06:26.986
All right.

116
00:06:26.986 --> 00:06:29.922
You'll be seeing some examples of these
in the upcoming section.

117
00:06:29.922 --> 00:06:31.023
So let's get back to it.

118
00:06:32.592 --> 00:06:35.595
Now let's
show an example of modifying the state.

119
00:06:36.028 --> 00:06:38.931
So let's start a new thread
and let's ask it what's the weather

120
00:06:38.931 --> 00:06:41.934
in LA?

121
00:06:44.704 --> 00:06:46.372
At this point in the thread

122
00:06:46.372 --> 00:06:49.809
we have two messages the human message
and then the AI message

123
00:06:50.042 --> 00:06:53.946
which is saying to search Tavily
for current weather in Los Angeles.

124
00:06:54.414 --> 00:06:55.548
But let's modify this.

125
00:06:55.548 --> 00:06:58.584
Let's pretend we were instead
asking about the weather in

126
00:06:58.584 --> 00:07:01.587
Louisiana, not Los Angeles.

127
00:07:01.587 --> 00:07:04.223
So how would we modify this to correct

128
00:07:04.223 --> 00:07:07.226
the agent action?

129
00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:10.663
First,
let's save the current state of the graph

130
00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:13.733
to a variable called current values.

131
00:07:16.936 --> 00:07:20.940
The last message we have in
this state is this AI message,

132
00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:24.043
which is saying to search
for a particular search term,

133
00:07:24.544 --> 00:07:26.913
in this case,
current weather in Los Angeles.

134
00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:32.585
We can drill in even further

135
00:07:32.585 --> 00:07:36.155
into see the list of tool calls
associated with this message.

136
00:07:38.391 --> 00:07:41.027
Let's now update these tool calls.

137
00:07:41.027 --> 00:07:45.598
In order to do that, we can first
get the ID associated with the tool call.

138
00:07:45.798 --> 00:07:49.335
Well then update
the tool calls property to be a list.

139
00:07:49.802 --> 00:07:51.370
This list will have one element.

140
00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:52.705
It's a dictionary.

141
00:07:52.705 --> 00:07:53.873
It's got one tool call.

142
00:07:53.873 --> 00:07:56.108
It's calling the Tavily
search results json.

143
00:07:56.108 --> 00:07:58.978
That's the same as before,
but the arguments are different.

144
00:07:58.978 --> 00:08:02.648
This time we have query
with current weather in Louisiana.

145
00:08:04.183 --> 00:08:08.221
This doesn't actually do anything
until we call: update state on the graph.

146
00:08:08.621 --> 00:08:11.190
We're going to pass in the thread config.

147
00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:13.726
And this is so we know
which thread we're operating on.

148
00:08:13.726 --> 00:08:16.929
And we're going to pass in the new values
that we want to override with.

149
00:08:21.567 --> 00:08:22.568
If we get the current

150
00:08:22.568 --> 00:08:26.305
state of the graph now we can see that
we have this search term here.

151
00:08:26.539 --> 00:08:29.542
Current weather in Louisiana.

152
00:08:33.813 --> 00:08:36.916
If we continue from here,
we can see that it calls Tavily

153
00:08:37.149 --> 00:08:38.050
with the current weather

154
00:08:38.050 --> 00:08:41.921
in Louisiana and gets back some response
and then responds accordingly.

155
00:08:41.921 --> 00:08:44.924
The current weather in Louisiana shows
sunny conditions.

156
00:08:45.424 --> 00:08:47.627
We've now shown how we can modify

157
00:08:47.627 --> 00:08:50.963
the state of the graph
in order to control what the agent does.

158
00:08:51.197 --> 00:08:52.765
One important thing to note,

159
00:08:52.765 --> 00:08:56.502
is that we're actually keeping
a running list of all these states.

160
00:08:57.036 --> 00:09:01.073
So when we modify the state,
we actually created a new state.

161
00:09:01.073 --> 00:09:02.408
And that became the new state.

162
00:09:02.408 --> 00:09:05.711
And then every time we update it
with results from the nodes,

163
00:09:05.878 --> 00:09:08.881
it's actually creating a new state
one after the other.

164
00:09:09.115 --> 00:09:12.685
This is really nice
because it actually allows us to go back

165
00:09:12.852 --> 00:09:16.522
and visit previous states in
something we're calling time travel.

166
00:09:18.491 --> 00:09:20.026
So in order to do this,

167
00:09:20.026 --> 00:09:24.163
we can call get state history
on the graph passing in again

168
00:09:24.163 --> 00:09:29.163
the thread ID. We'll then start building up
this list of states over time.

169
00:09:35.374 --> 00:09:37.009
If we get the last state

170
00:09:37.009 --> 00:09:40.513
in this list, we can see that it's
actually the earliest one.

171
00:09:40.813 --> 00:09:43.916
And so this is the one where
it was looking up the current weather

172
00:09:43.916 --> 00:09:44.850
in Los Angeles.

173
00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:49.188
This was the original state update
that was made based on the first language

174
00:09:49.188 --> 00:09:50.623
model call.

175
00:09:50.623 --> 00:09:54.193
If we wanted to go back in time
and resume from this checkpoint,

176
00:09:54.460 --> 00:09:57.496
where it was looking for Los Angeles,
we easily can.

177
00:09:58.364 --> 00:10:01.133
In order to do that, all we have to do,

178
00:10:01.133 --> 00:10:04.203
is call graph dot stream again,
pass in none.

179
00:10:04.704 --> 00:10:08.975
And now notice that we're passing in to
replay dot config.

180
00:10:09.141 --> 00:10:12.645
So to_replay
is the state where we want to resume from.

181
00:10:12.845 --> 00:10:15.414
And config
is just the configuration parameters

182
00:10:15.414 --> 00:10:18.184
that tell us
that we're resuming from this state.

183
00:10:18.184 --> 00:10:20.786
So if we run this, we'll start to see

184
00:10:20.786 --> 00:10:23.656
that it's searching
for the current weather in Los Angeles.

185
00:10:23.656 --> 00:10:26.359
It's getting a result back from Tavily,

186
00:10:26.359 --> 00:10:28.494
and then it's generating a response.

187
00:10:28.494 --> 00:10:29.729
And that's our final answer.

188
00:10:30.963 --> 00:10:32.264
One thing we can also do,

189
00:10:32.264 --> 00:10:35.368
is go back in time
and then edit it from there.

190
00:10:36.335 --> 00:10:39.071
So here we have this to_replay config.

191
00:10:39.071 --> 00:10:42.074
And this is where it's
the current weather in Los Angeles.

192
00:10:42.341 --> 00:10:45.011
So we can do the same thing that we did
before

193
00:10:45.011 --> 00:10:48.014
where we modify the state.

194
00:10:50.950 --> 00:10:53.419
And so we'll modify this
to do current weather

195
00:10:53.419 --> 00:10:57.356
and LA AccuWeather presuming
that we want a response from AccuWeather.

196
00:10:57.723 --> 00:11:00.726
And the reason
this is different than before is that here,

197
00:11:00.726 --> 00:11:04.597
we're going back in time
and then editing as opposed to before

198
00:11:04.597 --> 00:11:07.600
where we were editing
from the most recent state.

199
00:11:09.101 --> 00:11:11.037
We can update the state of this

200
00:11:11.037 --> 00:11:14.240
to_replay
and we'll get back this branch state.

201
00:11:14.774 --> 00:11:17.777
What we've branched off
with this modification.

202
00:11:21.147 --> 00:11:21.781
If we now

203
00:11:21.781 --> 00:11:25.551
call graph dot stream
with none on this branch state, we can see

204
00:11:25.551 --> 00:11:28.754
that we're looking up AccuWeather
and getting a result from there.

205
00:11:28.854 --> 00:11:30.456
And we're getting back a new answer.

206
00:11:30.456 --> 00:11:32.792
And then we're responding from the AI.

207
00:11:35.928 --> 00:11:37.296
Another thing we can do,

208
00:11:37.296 --> 00:11:40.933
is add messages to a state
at any given point in time.

209
00:11:41.467 --> 00:11:44.770
So here we have this to replay config
which we've modified

210
00:11:44.770 --> 00:11:47.773
to have the current weather
in LA, AccuWeather.

211
00:11:48.007 --> 00:11:51.210
Now let's presume that instead of actually
calling Tavily,

212
00:11:51.343 --> 00:11:53.846
we wanted to mock out a response.

213
00:11:53.846 --> 00:11:56.849
We can do that
by appending a new message into the state.

214
00:11:58.451 --> 00:12:00.986
We're going to grab the ID

215
00:12:00.986 --> 00:12:03.989
of the tool call
that we're supposed to be making.

216
00:12:05.257 --> 00:12:09.028
And we're going to create this state
update, which is a list of messages.

217
00:12:09.261 --> 00:12:10.429
And it's a new message

218
00:12:10.429 --> 00:12:12.998
this time. It's got a tool call ID of this.

219
00:12:12.998 --> 00:12:14.600
It's got a name of Tavily Search.

220
00:12:14.600 --> 00:12:17.603
And it's got a content of 54°C.

221
00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:19.739
So here this is a new message.

222
00:12:19.739 --> 00:12:23.576
So when we update the state in the graph,
it's not going to replace

223
00:12:23.576 --> 00:12:24.710
an existing message,

224
00:12:24.710 --> 00:12:27.146
it's going to append
it to the list of messages.

225
00:12:28.514 --> 00:12:30.883
We're now
going to update the state of the graph.

226
00:12:30.883 --> 00:12:34.887
But because we are actually adding
and pretending that an action has taken

227
00:12:34.887 --> 00:12:37.890
place, as opposed
to modifying the existing state,

228
00:12:38.390 --> 00:12:40.192
we need to do an additional thing.

229
00:12:40.192 --> 00:12:44.530
we need to add this as node equals action
parameter here.

230
00:12:44.964 --> 00:12:47.867
What this is basically doing
is it's saying that

231
00:12:47.867 --> 00:12:51.504
the state update that we're making isn't
just a modification.

232
00:12:51.737 --> 00:12:55.407
We're actually making this update
as if we were the action node.

233
